From 4f5820f147414adb367643407d6c91a66011988d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bjorn Pagen <11238136+bjornpagen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 21:15:10 -0600
Subject: [PATCH v1 3/4] Replan a cached generic plan when the plan advice in
 force changes.

pg_plan_advice applies advice when a query is planned, so a cached generic
plan went on being reused under whatever advice was in force when it was
made.  This was reported during review (Noah Misch) and documented as a
limitation, with DISCARD PLANS as the remedy.  But the plans most affected
are ones no user statement holds: ri_triggers.c prepares the queries for
each foreign key once and keeps them for the life of the backend.  So the
plan a foreign-key check runs depended on which transaction first ran that
check in the backend.  Measured on a referencing table of 60,000 rows that
statistics say is empty, the "is the deleted key still referenced?" check
read 12,424 buffers in a transaction whose advice pinned the key index,
because an earlier transaction without advice had cached a plan that
skip-scans a sibling index; one descent reads 2.  The reverse also held:
advice set with SET LOCAL kept steering that check after its transaction
ended.  Nothing reported either.

Add check_cached_plan_hook to plancache.c, consulted where search_path is
checked before a generic plan is reused (CheckCachedPlan and
CachedPlanIsSimplyValid); returning false invalidates the plan, which is
then rebuilt as after any invalidation.  The query tree doesn't depend on
the hook's inputs, so only the plan is rebuilt.

pg_plan_advice uses it to maintain one invariant: a generic plan is reused
only while the advice that would be supplied for its query now equals the
advice it was planned under, whether pg_plan_advice.advice or an advisor
supplies it.  Each PlannedStmt records the advice, its source, and a
generation for each kind of source, and each source is checked the way it
changes.  The GUC's generation is bumped by its assign hook; an unchanged
generation is one comparison, and a changed one costs a string
comparison, so a function whose SET clause sets the same string on every
call never replans because of it.  Advisors may now provide a generation
function, which pg_stash_advice does from its existing shared change
counter, its readiness flag and a count of assignments to
pg_stash_advice.stash_name; advisors are consulted again only when a
generation has moved, or when an advisor has none.  When the advice turns
out unchanged, the record's generations are updated in place, so the
check is paid once per change, not once per reuse.  Adding or removing an
advisor invalidates every cached plan.

This changes the advisor interface: pg_plan_advice_add_advisor() takes a
generation function (which may be NULL), and an advisor may now be called
with NULL glob and es, to revalidate a cached plan, and must then return
the advice it would supply if the query were planned at that moment.

The DISCARD PLANS limitation leaves the documentation.  New tests cover
a foreign-key check under force_generic_plan that follows advice set
after it was planned, the same in the default mode after the custom-plan
trials, advice that doesn't outlive its transaction, a function SET
clause that doesn't cause replanning, and a prepared statement that
follows a change to the stash.

