+Cached Plans
+============
+
+Advice is applied when a query is planned, but a generic plan can be cached
+and reused long after: a prepared statement, a statement in a PL/pgSQL
+function, or one of the queries ri_triggers.c prepares for each foreign key
+and keeps for the life of the backend. If such a plan went on being reused
+regardless of the advice in force, the plan a foreign-key check runs would
+depend on which transaction happened to run that check first in the backend,
+and advice set for one transaction would outlive it. So a generic plan is
+reused only while the advice that would be supplied for its query now equals
+the advice it was planned under, whatever supplies it.
+
+Core gives us check_cached_plan_hook, consulted where plancache.c checks
+search_path before reusing a generic plan. Each PlannedStmt records the advice
+it was planned under, whether an advisor or pg_plan_advice.advice supplied it,
+and a generation for each kind of source. Each source is checked the way it
+actually changes: an unchanged generation for the GUC (bumped by its assign
+hook) is a single comparison, and a changed one costs a string comparison, so
+a function whose SET clause sets the same string on every call never replans
+because of it; advisors may provide a generation function, and only when one
+has moved, or an advisor has none, do we consult the advisors again, with no
+PlannerGlobal or ExplainState. When the advice turns out to be unchanged, the
+record's generations are updated in place, so that the check is paid once per
+change rather than once per reuse. Adding or removing an advisor invalidates
+every cached plan.
+
 Future Work
 ===========
 
