  </sect2>
 
+ <sect2 id="pgplanadvice-cached-plans">
+  <title>Cached Plans</title>
+
+  <para>
+   A query whose plan is cached, such as a prepared statement, a statement
+   within a <application>PL/pgSQL</application> function, or one of the
+   queries that <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> itself prepares to
+   check foreign keys, is planned under whatever advice is in force at the
+   time. A cached generic plan is reused only while the advice that would be
+   supplied for the query now, from <varname>pg_plan_advice.advice</varname>
+   or from another module such as <xref linkend="pgstashadvice"/>, is the
+   advice it was planned under; otherwise, the plan is rebuilt, just as it
+   would be after a change to a table it uses. So advice set by
+   <command>SET LOCAL</command> reaches plans cached in earlier transactions,
+   and stops applying to them once the transaction ends. Comparing the advice
+   is cheap: a function whose <literal>SET</literal> clause supplies advice
+   sets the same string on every call, and does not cause its plans to be
+   rebuilt.
+  </para>
+
+  <para>
+   Modules that supply advice through <filename>pg_plan_advice</filename>'s
+   advisor interface are consulted again before a cached generic plan is
+   reused, without the planner state they receive when a query is planned,
+   and must return the advice they would supply if the query were planned
+   then. A module can avoid being consulted each time by also providing a
+   generation function, which reports a number that increases whenever its
+   advice could have changed; see <filename>pg_plan_advice.h</filename>.
+  </para>
+ </sect2>
+
